WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.563
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Course Foundations
and Scope as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.563 --> 00:00:09.875
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:09.875 --> 00:00:17.196
NARRATOR: In this course, students will
explore puzzle solving and creation as a way

4
00:00:17.196 --> 00:00:22.947
of exploring human-inspired algorithm
development and the synergy of humans and

5
00:00:22.947 --> 00:00:25.562
computers to develop programmatic solutions.

6
00:00:25.562 --> 00:00:32.546
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
will analyze how a maze-solving program can be

7
00:00:32.546 --> 00:00:38.532
designed to mimic human backtracking rather
than using a purely optimal breadth-first

8
00:00:38.532 --> 00:00:39.031
search.

9
00:00:39.031 --> 00:00:46.055
NARRATOR: On screen, display a split-screen
graphic: left side shows a grid maze with a

10
00:00:46.055 --> 00:00:53.079
red optimal path, right side shows the same
maze with a winding human-like path that

11
00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:54.015
occasionally backtracks.

12
00:00:54.015 --> 00:01:00.197
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

13
00:01:00.197 --> 00:01:01.624
change the conclusion.

14
00:01:01.624 --> 00:01:07.515
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

15
00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:14.123
NARRATOR: Now define Logical Puzzles and
Constraint Satisfaction precisely enough that

16
00:01:14.123 --> 00:01:17.662
another learner could test the definition.

17
00:01:17.662 --> 00:01:24.884
NARRATOR: Many different types of puzzles will
be explored, including logical puzzles, mazes,

18
00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:27.662
optimization puzzles, and word puzzles.

19
00:01:27.662 --> 00:01:35.027
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A Sudoku
solver uses backtracking to assign digits 1-9

20
00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:40.287
to empty cells while maintaining row, column,
and box constraints.

21
00:01:40.287 --> 00:01:47.207
NARRATOR: On screen, show a 9x9 grid with
highlighted constraints (e.g., a red circle

22
00:01:47.207 --> 00:01:54.621
around a row) and a step-by-step animation of
a cell being filled based on eliminated

23
00:01:54.621 --> 00:01:55.115
options.

24
00:01:55.115 --> 00:02:01.783
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

25
00:02:01.783 --> 00:02:04.005
evidence would distinguish them.

26
00:02:04.005 --> 00:02:10.537
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

27
00:02:10.658 --> 00:02:17.135
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Maze Algorithms and Graph

28
00:02:17.135 --> 00:02:20.373
Traversal operates in a concrete sequence.

29
00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:28.143
NARRATOR: Graph algorithms operate on vertices
and edges to answer reachability, ordering,

30
00:02:28.143 --> 00:02:30.733
connectivity, and path questions.

31
00:02:30.733 --> 00:02:36.853
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
depth-first search explores one corridor until

32
00:02:36.853 --> 00:02:42.973
a dead end, then backtracks to try an
alternative route, mirroring human

33
00:02:42.973 --> 00:02:43.483
exploration.

34
00:02:43.483 --> 00:02:50.027
NARRATOR: On screen, render a graph maze with
nodes and edges, highlighting the DFS

35
00:02:50.027 --> 00:02:54.701
traversal path in blue and the backtracking
steps in gray.

36
00:02:54.701 --> 00:03:01.920
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

37
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.545
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

38
00:03:07.658 --> 00:03:13.788
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Optimization Puzzles and Heuristics and

39
00:03:13.788 --> 00:03:16.342
make the decision criteria explicit.

40
00:03:16.342 --> 00:03:23.045
NARRATOR: For each type of puzzle, students
will develop and implement both optimal

41
00:03:23.045 --> 00:03:27.686
solving algorithms and algorithms that mimic
human solving processes.

42
00:03:27.686 --> 00:03:32.907
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
traveling salesman puzzle uses

43
00:03:32.907 --> 00:03:39.173
nearest-neighbor heuristics to quickly
generate a route, even if it is not

44
00:03:39.173 --> 00:03:40.217
mathematically optimal.

45
00:03:40.217 --> 00:03:47.683
NARRATOR: On screen, display a city map with
cities as nodes and roads as edges, showing a

46
00:03:47.683 --> 00:03:52.514
heuristic path in green versus a shorter
optimal path in red.

47
00:03:52.514 --> 00:03:59.101
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

48
00:03:59.101 --> 00:04:00.748
move the goalposts.

49
00:04:00.748 --> 00:04:07.623
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

50
00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:14.078
NARRATOR: Apply Word Puzzles and String
Processing to a worked situation instead of

51
00:04:14.078 --> 00:04:17.008
treating it as an isolated definition.

52
00:04:17.008 --> 00:04:24.091
NARRATOR: Automata and formal languages
describe machines, accepted strings, and

53
00:04:24.091 --> 00:04:25.508
computational limits.

54
00:04:25.508 --> 00:04:31.556
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
crossword solver checks candidate words

55
00:04:31.556 --> 00:04:37.055
against a dictionary using prefix matching to
validate partial fills.

56
00:04:37.055 --> 00:04:43.608
NARRATOR: On screen, show a crossword grid
with a candidate word list scrolling on the

57
00:04:43.608 --> 00:04:47.539
side, highlighting matches that fit the
existing letter pattern.

58
00:04:47.539 --> 00:04:54.050
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

59
00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:55.445
at each step.

60
00:04:55.445 --> 00:05:00.258
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

61
00:05:00.362 --> 00:05:06.568
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in Algorithmic
Paradigms and Data Structures, including what

62
00:05:06.568 --> 00:05:09.671
improves and what becomes more difficult.

63
00:05:09.671 --> 00:05:16.104
NARRATOR: Graphs can be represented with
adjacency structures for traversal and path

64
00:05:16.104 --> 00:05:16.640
algorithms.

65
00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:23.086
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A stack
stores the sequence of moves in a maze solver,

66
00:05:23.086 --> 00:05:27.921
allowing the program to reverse steps when a
dead end is reached.

67
00:05:27.921 --> 00:05:34.403
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a stack data
structure with maze move coordinates being

68
00:05:34.403 --> 00:05:38.890
pushed and popped, synchronized with the maze
grid animation.

69
00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:45.175
NARRATOR: State the benefit, the cost, and the
condition under which the tradeoff is

70
00:05:45.175 --> 00:05:45.624
acceptable.

71
00:05:45.624 --> 00:05:53.015
NARRATOR: Making the tradeoff visible prevents
a local improvement from hiding a larger cost.

72
00:05:53.138 --> 00:05:59.564
NARRATOR: Stress-test the reasoning around
Human-Inspired vs. Optimal Approaches with an

73
00:05:59.564 --> 00:06:04.822
edge case and identify which assumption
controls the outcome.

74
00:06:04.822 --> 00:06:09.244
NARRATOR: Recurrences describe the cost of
recursive algorithms.

75
00:06:09.244 --> 00:06:16.193
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A word
puzzle solver might prioritize common letters

76
00:06:16.193 --> 00:06:22.072
first, just as humans do, rather than
exhaustively checking every permutation.

77
00:06:22.072 --> 00:06:29.088
NARRATOR: On screen, show a side-by-side
comparison: left side displays a decision tree

78
00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:35.563
for optimal search, right side shows a
branching path that prioritizes high-frequency

79
00:06:35.563 --> 00:06:36.103
letters.

80
00:06:36.103 --> 00:06:42.221
NARRATOR: Change one assumption at a time and
observe which part of the reasoning no longer

81
00:06:42.221 --> 00:06:42.603
holds.

82
00:06:42.603 --> 00:06:48.633
NARRATOR: The edge case reveals the boundary
of the claim without discarding the useful

83
00:06:48.633 --> 00:06:49.494
core idea.

84
00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:56.670
NARRATOR: Use Puzzle Generation and Human
Solvability to diagnose a failure by

85
00:06:56.670 --> 00:07:00.200
separating symptoms, causes, and corrective
action.

86
00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:07.155
NARRATOR: Finally, they will discuss and solve
puzzle hunts, which include puzzles without

87
00:07:07.155 --> 00:07:13.575
any instruction and apparent ambiguity, yet
are still uniquely solvable for humans.

88
00:07:13.575 --> 00:07:20.399
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A puzzle
generator validates each clue against a

89
00:07:20.399 --> 00:07:27.747
solution set to ensure no contradictions exist
and that the puzzle is uniquely solvable.

90
00:07:27.747 --> 00:07:35.310
NARRATOR: On screen, display a puzzle
generation pipeline: input constraints ->

91
00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:41.497
solver verification -> ambiguity check ->
final puzzle output.

92
00:07:41.497 --> 00:07:47.179
NARRATOR: Locate the first point where
observed behavior diverges from expected

93
00:07:47.179 --> 00:07:50.794
behavior, then test the smallest plausible
cause.

94
00:07:50.794 --> 00:07:56.706
NARRATOR: The diagnosis is strongest when
another person can reproduce the observation

95
00:07:56.706 --> 00:07:58.184
and the test.

96
00:07:58.298 --> 00:08:04.613
NARRATOR: Connect Puzzle Hunts and Ambiguity
Resolution to the earlier ideas and explain

97
00:08:04.613 --> 00:08:08.013
how the pieces work as one system.

98
00:08:08.013 --> 00:08:15.943
NARRATOR: OpenDSA presents data structures and
algorithms through executable visualizations

99
00:08:15.943 --> 00:08:17.529
and exercises.

100
00:08:17.529 --> 00:08:24.506
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A hunt
puzzle might use a series of interconnected

101
00:08:24.506 --> 00:08:30.985
clues where each answer reveals the next,
requiring solvers to infer connections without

102
00:08:30.985 --> 00:08:31.982
explicit rules.

103
00:08:31.982 --> 00:08:38.455
NARRATOR: On screen, show a puzzle hunt board
with interconnected clue cards, highlighting

104
00:08:38.455 --> 00:08:43.435
how solving one card unlocks the next through
inferred relationships.

105
00:08:43.435 --> 00:08:52.297
NARRATOR: Explain each connection explicitly
and verify that information retains the same

106
00:08:52.297 --> 00:08:54.513
meaning across representations.

107
00:08:54.513 --> 00:09:01.565
NARRATOR: The synthesis matters only if every
connection remains supported by the original

108
00:09:01.565 --> 00:09:02.107
evidence.

109
00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:07.715
NARRATOR: Finish with Practical Analysis
Checklist as a practical checklist that can

110
00:09:07.715 --> 00:09:09.550
guide a new analysis.

111
00:09:09.550 --> 00:09:15.083
NARRATOR: They will also write programs that
construct certain types of puzzles with human

112
00:09:15.083 --> 00:09:16.269
solvers in mind.

113
00:09:16.269 --> 00:09:22.788
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
checklist confirms that every clue is

114
00:09:22.788 --> 00:09:29.851
